# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: release-arm32-thread-less-sbcl — the armv7 release lane cannot build: Debian's armhf SBCL has no threads
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -

* Context

pjb, 2026-08-18: « fix arm32 so it's not manual on release tags ». Done —
=release:linux:arm32= now carries =*on-release-arm32= and is scheduled
automatically on every =release-*= tag, with a =SKIP_ARM32_RELEASE=1=
opt-out like the two intermittent platforms.

Making it run revealed that it cannot succeed. Every earlier "success" of
this lane was an illusion: a guard in its =before_script= did =exit 0=,
which ended the whole job green in 35 seconds without building anything
(fixed in 1.8.47). The first real attempt, on the =release-1.8.47= tag,
ran for *44 minutes* and then failed.

* Problem

#+begin_example
Unhandled LOAD-SYSTEM-DEFINITION-ERROR: Error while trying to load
definition for system bordeaux-threads from pathname
/root/quicklisp/dists/quicklisp/software/bordeaux-threads-v0.9.4/bordeaux-threads.asd:
This implementation is unsupported.
#+end_example

Debian's armhf SBCL is built *without threads*. bordeaux-threads v0.9.4
refuses to load on a thread-less implementation outright — not a runtime
degradation, a load-time error — so nothing that depends on it can be
built, and clautolisp does.

The lane's own comment already anticipated the thread-less runtime:

#+begin_quote
SBCL only: armhf SBCL is thread-less (Debian), which clautolisp
tolerates — the default debugger is synchronous in-image; only the
threaded aldb session mode would error if used.
#+end_quote

That is true of clautolisp itself and false of its dependency graph: the
build never gets far enough to care what clautolisp tolerates.

CCL is not an alternative here — =armcl= faults under qemu-user on
poseidon, which is why =RELEASE_LISPS= is sbcl-only on this lane.

* Consequences

=.on-release-arm32= sets =allow_failure: true=, unlike the Windows and
macOS anchors which set it to =false=. Those two platforms are
*intermittent* — the machine is either there or the person cutting the
release declares it absent — so a failure is a real failure. This lane is
*blocked*, and a mandatory arm32 would block every release for a reason no
CI setting can fix.

So the release set is 15 of 17 artefacts whenever this is unresolved: the
=binaries-linux-arm32= / =libraries-linux-arm32= pair is missing, the
collect logs which targets actually reached it, and the release ships.

* Options

1. *Native armv7 hardware* (a Raspberry Pi runner). The lane's =tags:=
   comment already invites it: "native Pi welcome later". Does not by
   itself fix the threads question — Debian's armhf SBCL is thread-less on
   real hardware too.
2. *A threaded armhf SBCL*: build SBCL from source with
   =:sb-thread= on armv7. Whether upstream supports it on 32-bit ARM needs
   checking before any work is planned.
3. *Drop the bordeaux-threads dependency on thread-less builds.* Find what
   pulls it in and whether it is conditional. If clautolisp genuinely
   tolerates a thread-less runtime, the dependency ought to be
   conditional too — this is the option that also helps any other
   thread-less platform.
4. *Retire the lane.* If armv7 has no user, the honest move is to delete
   the job rather than ship a permanently red one.

Option 3 is worth investigating first: it is the only one that costs no
hardware and would tell us something true about the dependency graph.

* Not to be confused with

The lane's *other* two faults, both already fixed in 1.8.47:

- the =exit 0= guard that ended the job green without building
  (=before_script= and =script= share one shell);
- =collect:release= skipping itself because this job was manual and
  unplayed — =optional: true= covers a need that is ABSENT, not one that
  is present but unplayed.
